      <h1>How to hide photos on Android without an app</h1>
      <p>
        You can keep many private photos out of the main gallery without installing a third-party
        vault. The honest catch is that “hidden” is not the same as “encrypted,” and the built-in
        options differ by Android version and manufacturer. This guide sticks to methods that ship
        with Google Photos or the system, cites Google’s own docs, and ends with a short checklist
        for when a local encrypted vault is the better fit.
      </p>

      <h2>1. Google Photos Locked Folder</h2>
      <p>
        On Android 6 and later, Google Photos can move sensitive items into a
        <strong>Locked Folder</strong>. According to
        <a href="https://support.google.com/photos/answer/10694388?hl=en">Google Photos Help</a>,
        items there do not appear in the Photos grid, Memories, search, or albums, and they are not
        available to other apps that can read your photos. Unlocking uses your
        <strong>device screen lock</strong> — you cannot set a separate Locked Folder password.
      </p>
      <p>Typical steps (wording follows Google’s help article):</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Open the Google Photos app.</li>
        <li>Tap <strong>Collections</strong>, then <strong>Locked</strong>.</li>
        <li>Set up Locked Folder if prompted, and authenticate with your screen lock.</li>
        <li>Select photos or videos, then choose <strong>Add to → Move to Locked Folder</strong>.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Google also documents optional backup for Locked Folder. If backup is off, items live only
        on that device; uninstalling Photos, clearing app data, or losing the phone can wipe them.
        If backup is on, you can open Locked Folder on other signed-in devices with that device’s
        screen lock. Anyone who knows the phone lock can open the folder on that phone.
      </p>

      <h2>2. Android 15 Private Space (apps and files)</h2>
      <p>
        Starting with <strong>Android 15</strong>, many phones include
        <strong>Private space</strong>: a separate profile that can hide apps and hold files away
        from the main user. Google’s
        <a href="https://support.google.com/android/answer/15341885?hl=en">Android Help page</a>
        describes setup under Settings → Security &amp; privacy → Private space, a separate lock,
        and an option to hide the Private space entry when it is locked.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can move or copy files into Private space with <strong>Add → Add files</strong>.
        <strong>Move</strong> deletes the original from the main profile;
        <strong>Copy</strong> leaves the original in place. When Private space is locked, its apps
        stop, drop out of search and Recents, and leave the photo picker and sharing surfaces.
      </p>
      <p>Limits that matter for photos:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Requires Android 15+, supporting hardware, and the main user (not guest or secondary).</li>
        <li>
          Google states the space can still be discovered via computer/adb access, device logs, or
          other apps in some cases — so it is not forensic deniability.
        </li>
        <li>
          On-device Private space data is not part of a normal device backup restore. Deleting the
          space removes apps and data with no local backup.
        </li>
        <li>
          Google recommends a <strong>dedicated Google Account</strong> inside Private space so
          synced photos and history do not leak into the main profile.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Private space is strongest when you install a gallery or vault <em>inside</em> the space and
        keep the space locked. On its own it isolates files; it does not replace file-level
        encryption with a secret only you hold.
      </p>

