      <h2>3. OEM “secure folder” and gallery hide tools</h2>
      <p>
        Samsung Secure Folder, Xiaomi Second space, and other manufacturer tools can hide albums or
        whole workspaces. Steps and strength vary by brand. Treat them like Private space: useful
        for shared-phone moments, still tied to the vendor account and device lock model. Check your
        maker’s support pages for the exact menu path on your build.
      </p>

      <h2>4. Weak tricks that are not protection</h2>
      <p>
        Moving images into an obscure folder, renaming them, or relying on a
        <code>.nomedia</code> file only changes what some galleries index. A file manager, USB
        connection, or backup tool can still read the bytes. Do not treat this as privacy for IDs,
        medical images, or anything you would not leave in Downloads.
      </p>

      <h2>5. Checklist: is “without an app” enough?</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Who can unlock the phone? Locked Folder and most OEM tools use that same lock.</li>
        <li>Did cloud backup already keep a copy outside the hidden place?</li>
        <li>Do messaging apps still hold a compressed duplicate?</li>
        <li>Can you recover the files if Photos is cleared or Private space is deleted?</li>
        <li>Do you need a secret that is not the device PIN (passphrase, separate vault PIN)?</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        If you need encryption under a vault secret, optional delete of gallery originals after
        import, and an encrypted backup file you control, that is the job of a vault app — not a
        renamed folder.
      </p>

      <h2>Where Vireault fits</h2>
